title A law that governed three languages, and a meter that read one -- 10,468 non-ASCII characters brought to 505
prompt Autonomous loop -- roster cold first, Standfast on reds, next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, rota row N mod 5, condense ITINERARY and refill Now, send each finished increment
think Ran the cold roster to completion BEFORE touching a file, which is the correction to my own process red of the previous lap -- that one started the roster in the background and edited the tree while it was still walking, so it reported a red the lap had itself created. 58 guards, 58 green, 0 red, run_verdict=ok on an untouched tree.
think Every living row in construction/REDS.md reads CLOSED, so no standfast is in force and constructive work may open.
think Lap index from git log --oneline | wc -l = 3427, so the council rota reads row 3427 mod 5 = 2 -- Fire and Mars, cut and stop: standfast, reds-first, and the three depths of removal. The row governed the lap's shape directly. Standfast asks that a fix address why the defect entered rather than the mark it left, and the defect here is not the 10,468 characters; it is that a law reaching three languages had a meter reading one.
think Took the previous lap's recommend at its second item. It named two doors: caravan_suite_witness standing off the roster, and the ASCII guard reading *.rye alone while .rish and .sh comments sit outside it. Chose the second on Lindy weight -- a guard that holds a class of defect out of two whole languages outlives a roster row -- and on crux weight, since the hard part is a real correctness question about what a comment is in shell.
think Measured before believing, and the census decided the whole lap. 10,471 non-ASCII characters stand in # comments across 2,823 tracked .rish and .sh files, and 10,002 of them -- 95.5% -- are the exact six forms the rule's own substitution table already names. That is a different population from the Rye remainder, which is notation; this one is punctuation the rule has an answer for.
think The shape question was whether to generalize the Rye scan or write a sibling. Chose the sibling. The two meters share a file-list loop of about fifteen lines and differ in the substance -- Rye spells a comment // and must refuse a \\ multiline string, Rishi and shell spell it # and must refuse a heredoc body. One body wearing a flag would be two bodies wearing an if, and each would be harder to read and to prove. REDS %215's lesson is about a body shared by COPYING; these are not copies.
think The heredoc is the whole safety question, and I measured its size before designing for it: exactly 3 characters tree-wide sit inside a heredoc body, in tools/fixtures/link_witness_scan.sh (a Python program) and tools/w/wov_tb_repl_lab.sh. Small, and the guarantee is the point -- the same distinction the Rye meter draws at a string literal, for the same reason. The scan's 10,468 equals the naive 10,471 minus those 3, which is the exclusion proven by arithmetic before it was proven by a plant.
think Tightened the delimiter rule to the shell's own before writing the control: <<-WORD strips leading tabs so its delimiter may be indented, and plain <<WORD requires column zero. The loose form would close a heredoc early, which reads program content as prose -- the wrong direction. Re-measured after the change and the tree read 505 either way, so the strictness cost nothing here and is right anyway.
think Wrote the converter to write through the original inode -- cat "$tmp" > "$f" rather than mv -- because a file's mode is content the repository tracks (.claude/rules/exec-bit.md, thirty-nine files dropped to 100644 in one repoint pass). Proved it: git diff --summary reports 0 mode changes across 2,163 files.
obs The control earned itself twice over, and the second time is the one worth keeping. It found that a here-string <<<"x" contains <<"x" starting one character in, so awk's match() found an opener there and swallowed the rest of the file. Then it found my own ceiling extraction reading empty, because a greedy .*ceiling= runs past ceiling= and lands on under_ceiling=, whose value is yes rather than a digit. Neither was visible in the passing direction.
obs What the here-string fault teaches is larger than the fault. This tree holds ZERO here-strings in 2,823 tracked .rish and .sh files, so the corrected converter changed 0 further files and the reading stayed at 505. No sample of real content could have found it, at any size, because the construction does not occur here. A plant reproduces a shape the tree has yet to write, and that is the difference between a control and a spot check.
obs Proved the ceiling from both sides, which the sibling Rye control does not do. The pen is pushed one character past the scan's own ceiling -- read from the scan's output rather than spelled in the control, so it stays true when a lap lowers it -- and must read under_ceiling=no; then the plant is removed and it must return to green. A refusal proven only in the passing direction cannot be told from a bypass.
obs Left the roster at 58 rows on purpose. One law wants one guard, so tools/as/ascii_comment_witness.rish now holds both meters and reads twenty behaviors rather than seven. A second roster row would have said there are two laws.
obs Updated the canonical .claude/rules/ascii-first.md alone and left the .cursor twin untouched. That pair is one of the 36 the card names as a custody gate (REDS %194), and reading it showed the drift is deliberate: the Cursor side is a compressed short form that names the .claude side as canonical. Adding a true sentence to the full form is docs-implementation-sync; merging the pair is Keaton's.
obs Graded every artifact the lap touched. The rule reads B+ at 88, the scan, the control and the witness each read A+ at 100. Nothing below B, so no molt frame was pushed and the sweep ran to its own close.
loom witness=ascii_comment readings=20 controls=2 plants=13 ceiling_sides=2
